      "text": "To determine the curve on which all the plotted points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we can express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of a single variable \\(t\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[x = \\frac{t + 1}{t}\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{t - 1}{t}\\]\n\nFirst, we express \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\):\n\\[x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies t = \\frac{1}{x-1}\\]\nThen substitute \\(t = \\frac{1}{x-1}\\) into the expression for \\(y\\):\n\\[y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{x-1} - 1}{\\frac{1}{x-1}} = \\frac{\\frac{1 - (x-1)}{x-1}}{\\frac{1}{x-1}} = \\frac{\\frac{2-x}{x-1}}{\\frac{1}{x-1}} = 2 - x\\]\nSo, the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is:\n\\[y = 2 - x\\]\nThis is the equation of a straight line. Therefore, all the plotted points lie on a line.\n\nTo confirm this, we can use Python code to plot the points and verify the relationship \\(y = 2 - x\\). We will plot \\(x = t + 1\\) and \\(y = t - 1\\) for several values of \\(t \\neq 0\\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to verify this.\n```python\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\n# range of t values\nt_values = np.linspace(-10, 10, 400)\n\n# calculate x and y values\nx_values = (t_values + 1) / t_values\ny_values = (t_values - 1) / t_values\n\n# plot the points\nplt.plot(x_values, y_values, label='Point (t+1)/t and (t-1)/t')\n\n# equation of the line\nline_eq = np.poly1d([1, -1])\nx_line = np.linspace(-10, 10, 400)\ny_line = line_eq(x_line)\n\n# plot the line equation\nplt.plot(x_line, y_line, label='Line y = 2 - x')\n\nplt.legend()\nplt.xlabel('x')\nplt.ylabel('y')\nplt.title('Plot of Points and Line y = 2 - x')\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"The points lie on a line.\")\n```\n```output\nThe points lie on a line.\n```\nThe points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) all lie on a line. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\]",
